WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.523 --> 00:00:09.688
Three thousand years ago, the ancient Greeks introduced a new sports competition.

2
00:00:09.689 --> 00:00:14.435
There were many athletic contests in ancient Greece, but this one was different.

3
00:00:14.436 --> 00:00:24.086
For a start, this competition honored the mighty Zeus, ruler of all Greek gods, so it included not only sports, but religious celebration as well.

4
00:00:24.087 --> 00:00:33.559
For the next thousand years, it attracted the best athletes from Greek city-states, from places as far away as modern-day Ukraine, Egypt, and Turkey.

5
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:42.407
The first of these contests took place in the Elis region of Greece, at a sacred spot called Olympia, in 776 BC.

6
00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:49.049
The competition was so successful that the Greeks continued to hold similar events every four years.

7
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:55.812
They called these competitions the Olympic Games. So why were the Games so special?

8
00:00:55.813 --> 00:00:59.638
Exercise was very important in the ancient Greek world.

9
00:00:59.639 --> 00:01:06.394
The Greeks believed in a well-rounded education, emphasizing exercise for the body as well as the mind.

10
00:01:06.395 --> 00:01:11.219
Moreover they believed that men should stay in shape in case they had to serve in the army.

11
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.450
To promote health, every Greek city had a gymnasium and a wrestling school called a palaestra.

12
00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:26.699
These places were so important to each community that they also became social centers, where citizens could meet to discuss politics or listen to educational lectures.

13
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:35.359
The Olympics celebrated the importance of fitness in Greek society by providing a forum for the best athletes to come together and flaunt their skills.

14
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.851
These people proudly represented their local communities.

15
00:01:38.852 --> 00:01:45.353
If someone from Athens, for example, won an event, he brought honor to his entire city-state.

16
00:01:45.354 --> 00:01:49.083
The Olympic Games also had some political benefits for the Greeks.

17
00:01:49.084 --> 00:01:57.511
In the months before the Games started, messengers went from city to city announcing the dates of the event and that the Olympic Truce was to begin.

18
00:01:57.512 --> 00:02:06.003
The Olympic Truce stated that anyone involved in the Games must be allowed safe passage to Olympia, even through hostile enemy territory.

19
00:02:06.004 --> 00:02:15.148
The Olympic Games brought together residents of numerous city-states, allowing them to discuss politics, make alliances, and even negotiate peace.

20
00:02:15.149 --> 00:02:19.900
Meanwhile athletes were training hard for the upcoming games with their coaches.

21
00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:28.971
Each city selected its best athletes to travel to Elis one month ahead of the Games to train on site, where they stayed in huge tents.

22
00:02:28.972 --> 00:02:36.550
During this period Olympic officials, who chose which athletes qualified to compete, evaluated all hopeful contestants.

23
00:02:36.551 --> 00:02:43.805
Those selected competed individually, not as part of a team, and competition was limited to Greek men.

24
00:02:43.806 --> 00:02:48.643
Slaves, foreigners, and unmarried women could only participate as spectators.

25
00:02:48.644 --> 00:02:53.705
They joined the thousands of Greek men who traveled from far away to see the Games.

26
00:02:53.706 --> 00:03:00.203
As many as fifty thousand spectators and merchants attended the Games, camping outside under the stars.

27
00:03:00.204 --> 00:03:04.044
Married women, however, were prohibited from viewing the Games.

28
00:03:04.045 --> 00:03:09.188
If one was caught on the grounds, she was thrown off a nearby mountain as punishment!

29
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:13.605
The Olympic Festival began with an opening ceremony in a temple.

30
00:03:13.606 --> 00:03:18.184
This rite included the sacrifice of one hundred heads of cattle to honor Zeus.

31
00:03:18.185 --> 00:03:25.031
Afterwards poets and playwrights presented their works, the crowds cheered, and the Games began.

32
00:03:25.032 --> 00:03:31.681
All events took place either in the stadium or the hippodrome, a track for racing horses.

33
00:03:31.682 --> 00:03:39.752
The very first Olympic Games lasted only three days, but by the fifth century BC, they had been extended to five days.

34
00:03:39.753 --> 00:03:44.153
The spectators sat on the ground, while officials watched from tents.

35
00:03:44.154 --> 00:03:52.677
At first the main event was a footrace, but over time more competitions were added, such as boxing, wrestling, and chariot racing.

36
00:03:52.678 --> 00:03:56.479
The events back then were a little different from their modern counterparts.

37
00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.976
Boxing, for example, had no rule against hitting an opponent while he was down.

38
00:04:00.977 --> 00:04:06.812
The footrace sometimes became so violent that the athletes were forced to wear helmets and carry shields.

39
00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:13.312
Another modern development is uniforms; in the ancient Olympics, athletes competed totally nude.

40
00:04:13.313 --> 00:04:20.652
They did this because the Greeks had a deep appreciation for the human body and believed that displaying it was a tribute to Zeus.

41
00:04:20.653 --> 00:04:32.459
Another difference between the ancient and modern Olympics is that there was only one winner in each eventㅡthere was no second or third place─and he received an olive branch as his prize instead of a medal.

42
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:38.544
This may not seem like much, but a victorious athlete could live off his achievements for the rest of his life.

43
00:04:38.545 --> 00:04:45.943
His hometown might reward him with food, cash, honorary appointments, or leadership positions in the community.

44
00:04:45.944 --> 00:04:52.544
Greek writers, poets, and sculptors also recorded his story, so that his victory was never forgotten.

45
00:04:52.545 --> 00:04:59.018
One such athlete was the boxer Melankomas of Caria, a town in modern Turkey.

46
00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:02.103
He won the boxing event in 49 AD.

47
00:05:02.104 --> 00:05:08.268
He was famous not only for his magnificent body and good looks, but also for his unusual fighting technique.

48
00:05:08.269 --> 00:05:14.758
Melankomas did not strike his opponents; rather, he wore them out by skillfully dodging their blows.

49
00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:20.177
It was said that he could fight all day long without tiring, even in the Greek heat.

50
00:05:20.178 --> 00:05:26.167
Another legendary athlete of the ancient world was Milo of Kroton, who was from what is now Italy.

51
00:05:26.168 --> 00:05:32.237
He won the wrestling event at six Olympic Games between 540 BC and 516 BC.

52
00:05:32.238 --> 00:05:36.488
Milo enjoyed showing off his unrivaled strength and control.

53
00:05:36.489 --> 00:05:40.667
He would hold a piece of fruit in his hand and challenge people to take it away from him.

54
00:05:40.668 --> 00:05:45.570
Even though he held the fruit so tightly that no one could take it, he never damaged it.

55
00:05:45.571 --> 00:05:54.403
Another of his favorite tricks was to tie a string around his head, hold his breath, and break the string with the veins bulging out of his forehead!

56
00:05:54.404 --> 00:05:58.965
Theagenes of Thasos became famous at the age of nine.

57
00:05:58.966 --> 00:06:05.304
One day, as he was walking home from school, he noticed a bronze statue of a god in the marketplace.

58
00:06:05.305 --> 00:06:10.476
For some inexplicable reason, Theagenes broke the statue off its base.

59
00:06:10.477 --> 00:06:16.037
The story of a young boy who could break bronze with his bare hands was talked about everywhere.

60
00:06:16.038 --> 00:06:22.625
When he was older, Theagenes competed in the Olympics and easily won the boxing prize.

61
00:06:22.626 --> 00:06:26.125
Other athletes were remembered for less honorable reasons.

62
00:06:26.126 --> 00:06:33.866
Even though all contestants in the Games took an oath to Zeus that they would abide by the rules, some did not keep this promise.

63
00:06:33.867 --> 00:06:40.440
One infamous cheater was Eupolus of Thessaly, who bribed other boxers in the 98th Olympics.

64
00:06:40.441 --> 00:06:47.774
In the 112th Olympics, Callippus of Athens gave money to all the other athletes in his race to let him win.

65
00:06:47.775 --> 00:06:51.951
Cheating was punished; dishonest athletes had to pay huge fines.

66
00:06:51.952 --> 00:06:59.999
With this money, statues of Zeus were erected along the road that led to the stadium, with the names of the cheats inscribed on their bases.

67
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.103
To get to the stadium, the athletes had to walk past all the statues, which reminded them of what not to do.

68
00:07:07.104 --> 00:07:14.341
The Olympic Games were so important in the ancient Greek world that time was not measured in years, but in Olympiads.

69
00:07:14.342 --> 00:07:19.332
One Olympiad is the amount of time between each Olympic Game, four years.

70
00:07:19.333 --> 00:07:27.459
Things changed, however, in 146 BC, when the Romans conquered Greece. They did not worship Zeus, the Romans were Christian.

71
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:37.639
In 393 AD Roman Emperor Theodosius I abolished the Olympic Games because they did not want their citizens celebrating a pagan god.

72
00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:46.965
It wasn't until the late nineteenth century that people started to think again about organizing an international sporting event and reinvented the Olympic Games.

73
00:07:46.966 --> 00:08:00.615
Today, almost two thousand years after the original Games ended, athletes from most countries in the world participate in the modern Olympics, a tribute to the lasting influence of the Ancient Greek heroes, their traditions, and eternal spirit.
